#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qb-start — start one agent session on a named command. The verb that was missing (#277).

  qb-start /fix-issue 277               start a session working issue 277
  qb-start /panel-review-pr 352         …reviewing PR 352
  qb-start --dry-run /fix-issue 277     every refusal, nothing started
  qb-start --repo-path ../other /review-pr 12

  0  started    — a real, attachable session exists (or: a clean dry run)
  2  used wrongly
  3  NOT ENABLED on this machine — the default, and the whole of it
  4  refused    — that command is not on this machine's allowlist
  5  refused    — this machine's cap is spent, or its panes could not be counted
  6  refused    — the shared window is spent, or `qb-pace` could not read it
  7  refused    — this repo's window is full, or `qb-admit` could not read it
  8  refused    — somebody holds that work, or the claim could not be taken at all
  9  could not start it — no tmux, tmux refused, or the pane could not be stamped.
                 The claim goes back, and it says so if that failed too
 10  refused    — the FLEET's ceiling is spent: too many agents are live across the
                 whole board. Nothing on this box changes that

**THE HALF OF #277 THAT WAS NEVER BUILT.** There were three ways to start a
session here and every one of them ended at a human hand: `qb-seat` in a pane
somebody typed into, `qb-dash-tui`'s ⚒ on a mouse click, and `run_agent`'s
headless `claude -p` inside a loop a person launched. v2.77 gave the fleet the
`end` verb. This is `start`, and it is deliberately the smallest thing that can
be one: a session that is **countable, claimable, endable, and off by default**.

**IT IS NOT A DISPATCHER, and that line is not being moved.** `qb-seat:44` says
the board coordinates work and does not operate the machine; what that sentence
is about is *dispatch*, and nothing here picks an item or tells an agent what to
work on. It is told a command by whatever pulled it, exactly as the dash's ⚒ is
told one by a click. Which work an agent takes stays the agent's own choice,
self-selected and claimed atomically — a spawner that read the plan and handed
seat 1 the first item would be hub-and-spoke with a hub that runs once, and
self-selection is the whole design.

**That sentence used to say "nothing here reads the plan", and #541 edited it
deliberately rather than crossing it quietly.** `/get-involved` takes no number,
so before spawning one this asks the board a single question — *is anything on
this repo's plan free* — and refuses if the answer is no. That is not picking:
nothing is passed to the agent, and the item it eventually claims may not be the
one that was free when the question was asked. It is what `qb-admit` already
asks, *is there room*, with no say in what goes in the space. The distinction
worth holding is between reading the plan to CHOOSE (still refused, still the
whole design) and reading it to decline early (this, and it costs a session that
would otherwise start, read the plan, find nothing and stop).

**THE BRIEF IS A NAMED COMMAND, NEVER FREE TEXT.** :data:`SPAWNABLE` is the whole
of what may be started and it is compiled in, so the spawn surface is an
allowlist rather than a prompt; a machine's policy file can only ever NARROW it.
That matters because this repo is public and anyone can open an issue: under a
board-sourced trigger, issue text becomes the instructions for an agent holding a
full shell, and the only mitigation that works is an allowlist — a filter is a
list of the phrasings somebody already thought of, and the attack is the one
nobody thought of (#63, and `issue_pickup._allowed_authors` says it first).

**OFF BY DEFAULT, AND THE DEFAULT COSTS NOTHING.** With no policy file this
refuses at exit 3 before it has looked for a board, a token, a network, tmux or
the agent binary — asserted in `harness/tests/test_qb_start.py` by handing it a
`qbdata` that raises on IMPORT, which is the bar #337 set for its own default.
A machine opts in by having `programs.quarterback-harness.spawn.enable` render
:data:`POLICY_NAME`; a repository cannot, and neither can an agent, because the
file lives in the user's config directory and the module writes it read-only.

**A MALFORMED POLICY FAILS CLOSED, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF `qb-admit`.** That is
not an inconsistency, it is the same principle applied to a switch that points
the other way. `in_flight.max` is a *restriction*: failing open on a typo admits
one agent too many, and failing closed would throttle every checkout on the fleet
over a config file — the worse morning, and the harder one to diagnose from a
phone. `spawn.json` is a *permission*: failing open on a typo starts sessions
nobody authorised, on a box holding `~/.claude`, `~/.config/gh` and the board
token. Unreadable, unparseable, or `enabled` anything other than `true` — all one
answer, and the answer is no.

**AND THE CEILING IS A DIAL, WHICH THE TWO ABOVE IT DELIBERATELY ARE NOT (#563).**
`spawn.json` carries three keys and only two of them are permissions. `enabled` and
`commands` say what this box MAY do, and they stay in the nix-written, read-only
file for the reason the paragraph above gives. `max_sessions` says how HARD it may
work, which is the `in_flight.max` side of the very line that paragraph draws: it
counts a resource rather than guarding a door. It was in the permission file because
that is where it was written, and it inherited the permission file's deployment path
— a nix edit, a build, a PR, a merge, a `nixos-rebuild` and a human with the
password, for a number. So the board's `spawn.max_sessions` answers first and the
file is the fallback; with no dial set this box behaves exactly as it did before.

Two things make that a throttle rather than a hole. An agent may read its own
ceiling and cannot raise it — which until #591 was a property of the GATE (`POST
/dials` took `Depends(human)`) and is now enforced HERE, because that endpoint also
accepts a delegated agent. `ceilings_from_board` honours only the three `set_via`
values that mean a person (`edge`, `key`, `dev`), plus a null one, which predates
the column; anything else is dropped with a line naming who set it. This is
the one dial whose subject is the agent reading it, so it is the one that cannot
follow the rest.

That is only half of it, and the half here is the weaker one. An agent that could
CLEAR a person's ceiling would raise its own without ever writing a bigger number —
a person sets 2, the file says 8, the dial goes away and 8 applies — because
"nobody set one" and "an agent removed the one somebody set" are the same empty
answer by the time it reaches this function. The other half is on the BOARD:
`app.api.dials` refuses a delegated agent replacing or clearing a person-authored
row. And the read
FAILS OPEN, unlike its neighbours in that file and for `qb-admit`'s reason: an
unreadable dial means the file's number applies, because this one is counting a
resource. A permission that failed open would start sessions nobody authorised; a
ceiling that failed closed would stop a box over a board hiccup, which is worse than
the thing it guards.

**AND THERE IS A SECOND CEILING, ACROSS THE WHOLE BOARD.** `spawn.max_sessions` is
counted here, on this tmux server, which is right — it is a property of the box, its
cores and whether somebody is sitting at it — and it is why five boxes each set to 3
have a fleet ceiling of fifteen and nothing that knows it. `spawn.max_sessions_fleet`
is that missing number: live agents across the board, counted off `GET /active`, with
no policy-file counterpart on purpose, because a per-machine file cannot express a
fleet-wide number and letting each box carry a copy is how five boxes come to
disagree about the limit.

It is a RUNAWAY GUARD AND NOT AN ALLOCATOR, and every property follows from that.
It is advisory and non-atomic — two boxes spawning in the same second can both see
room, exactly as `qb-admit` documents, and making it exact means moving admission
into the board, which is a bigger change than the failure being prevented deserves:
the failure is a hundred agents, not the hundred-and-first. It fails OPEN, alone
among the gates below, and the reason is that the local ceiling is the real safety
net: a board outage degrades five boxes from "ten across the board" to "five each",
which is bounded and nowhere near a runaway, while a fleet gate that failed closed
would stop every box spawning whenever the board hiccups. And it is unset by
default, because a ceiling that bites in normal use gets raised until it does not,
and then it is not a guard.

**IT COUNTS EVERY LIVE AGENT, NOT EVERY SPAWN**, and that is a real difference from
the ceiling above it. `GET /active` lists top-level sessions holding a lease
whatever opened them, so a seat somebody typed into consumes the fleet ceiling and a
spawned pane consumes both. That is the honest number for a runaway guard — a
hundred agents is a hundred agents, and the board cannot tell a spawn from a seat
without new plumbing at both ends — but it means a busy human day can refuse a
legitimate spawn, which is an argument for setting this one generously.

**WHAT IT STARTS IS A REAL SESSION IN A REAL PANE.** A detached tmux window, the
same shape `qb-dash-tui.run_in_window` already uses, so a human can attach to it,
read it, interrupt it and take it over. There are no hidden sessions here: a
spawn that could not be watched could not be stopped either, and #263 and #252
are both records of the board losing track of a session nobody could see.

**AND IT IS ENDABLE BEFORE IT EXISTS.** The session id is minted HERE and handed
to the agent with `--session-id`, so the pane carries `@qb_session` from the
moment it is created rather than from whenever the agent's SessionStart hook gets
round to it. `qb-end <that id>` works immediately, the seat bar's ✕ can reach it,
and the ordinary SessionEnd hook ends it with a reason like any other session.

**AND WHAT PULLED IT IS RECORDED.** `--via` names the caller — a person at a
prompt, the dashboard's ⚒ — and it lands on the claim note, on the board post
(spawns AND refusals) and on the pane as `@qb_spawn_via`. A session that appears
on a machine nobody is watching raises exactly one question, and this is the file
that has to be able to answer it. It is a closed set for :data:`SPAWNABLE`'s
reason: a provenance field the caller fills in freely is one that can be made to
say a human did it.

**THE CLAIM IS THE COUNT.** Taken before the process exists, through the ordinary
`qb-claim` path, on the resource the command names — so `qb-admit`'s window,
`GET /claim/held`, the plan and `qb-reconcile` all see a spawn as one unit of
work, and two spawns cannot both take one issue. It records **no session**, for
the reason `create-worktree`'s claim stanza gives at length: the agent that will
do the work does not exist yet, and a claim stamped with a session that is not
the worker's makes `may_mutate` refuse the worker its own renewal. So it is a
machine-held claim that the spawned session's own `create-worktree` renews, and
that the land step, the teardown and the sweep hand back (#337).

**ADVISORY, LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE IN THIS TABLE.** The cap and the window are
counted and then the claim is taken, so two `qb-start`s in the same second can
both see room. Same non-atomicity `qb-admit` documents, same reason it is not
fixed here: making it exact means moving admission into the board.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import uuid
from pathlib import Path
from typing import NamedTuple

#: The exits. 1 is deliberately unused: every other `qb-` tool spends it on "the
#: board decided against this", and here there are six distinct refusals whose
#: remedies differ — a caller that could not tell "not enabled on this machine"
#: from "somebody else has that issue" would retry the first forever and give up
#: on the second.
STARTED, MISUSE = 0, 2
NOT_ENABLED, NOT_ALLOWED, AT_CAP = 3, 4, 5
PACED, FULL, HELD = 6, 7, 8
COULD_NOT_START = 9
#: Its own code rather than a second flavour of `AT_CAP`, on this file's own rule:
#: the codes exist so a caller can tell refusals apart by REMEDY, and these two have
#: nothing in common there. `AT_CAP` is answered on this box — close a pane, or raise
#: this machine's number. This one cannot be: the fleet is at its ceiling and the
#: only things that move it are a dial nobody on this box can write and somebody
#: else's session ending.
AT_FLEET_CAP = 10

#: Every command that may EVER be spawned, and the resource each one claims.
#: Compiled in rather than configured, because a machine may narrow this and must
#: not be able to widen it: a policy file that could name any string would be the
#: free-text brief this exists to refuse, one indirection further away.
#:
#: The claim kind is here and not in the policy for the same reason. It is what
#: makes a spawn countable, and a command whose unit of work nobody has worked
#: out is a command that cannot be started — not one that is started uncounted.
#: `/investigate` is here for #63's sake and it is the SAFEST entry, not a
#: widening: it reads, writes up what it found, and implements nothing. Leaving
#: it out while `/fix-issue` was in had the ladder upside down — the issue
#: watcher's default rung is the read-only one precisely so that a close call
#: costs a writeup instead of a PR, and a spawn allowlist that admitted only the
#: rung that writes code would have made the safe answer the unstartable one.
class Spawnable(NamedTuple):
    """What a spawnable command takes, and what it can turn into.

    `kind` used to be the whole value here, and it was doing two jobs at once: it
    named the ref a number points at, and — because every entry had one — it was
    also the assertion that a number exists. `/get-involved` (#541) takes none, so
    the arity has to be said rather than implied. `kind is None` IS "no argument",
    and every site that used to read the ref kind now has to decide which of the
    two questions it was asking.

    `dispatches` is the set of commands this one can run in turn, and it exists
    for a permission reason rather than a documentary one — see :func:`admits`.
    Empty for everything that only does its own work.
    """

    kind: str | None
    dispatches: tuple[str, ...] = ()


SPAWNABLE = {
    "/investigate": Spawnable("issue"),
    "/fix-issue": Spawnable("issue"),
    "/fix-and-review": Spawnable("issue"),
    "/fix-and-land": Spawnable("issue"),
    "/review-pr": Spawnable("pr"),
    "/panel-review-pr": Spawnable("pr"),
    #: Takes no number: it reads the plan and self-selects (#541). That makes it
    #: the SAFEST entry on the axis this file worries about most — there is no
    #: attacker-controlled text to carry in, because there is no argument at all —
    #: and the most dangerous on the other lock, because it dispatches. What it
    #: can turn into is `harness/commands/get-involved.md`'s own table, and it is
    #: repeated here because :func:`admits` has to enforce it and a rule that
    #: reads a markdown file is not a rule.
    "/get-involved": Spawnable(
        None, ("/fix-issue", "/fix-and-land", "/review-pr", "/panel-review-pr")),
}


def admits(command: str, allowed: set[str]) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """May this machine spawn `command`, given the commands its policy allows?

    `(True, "")`, or `(False, why)`. The plain case is membership. The case this
    function exists for is composition: `/get-involved` dispatches into
    `/fix-issue`, `/fix-and-land`, `/review-pr` and `/panel-review-pr`, so a
    policy that allows it and deliberately withholds `/fix-issue` **gets
    `/fix-issue` anyway**, one hop along, on an issue chosen from the plan.

    That is not a leak to document, it is the second lock failing to mean what it
    says. This file's own claim is that a policy file *"can only ever NARROW"* the
    compiled set, and the operator who withheld `/fix-issue` has no way to see
    that it did not: `--policy` reports the allowlist, which would no longer be
    the set of things that can run. So the rule is enforced rather than described,
    and `--policy` reports the implication (#541).

    The alternative — document it and leave the check out — is a policy file that
    means something other than what it says, on the one gate whose entire job is
    meaning exactly what it says.
    """
    if command not in allowed:
        return False, f"{command} is not on this machine's allowlist"
    missing = sorted(set(SPAWNABLE[command].dispatches) - allowed)
    if missing:
        return False, (
            f"{command} dispatches into {', '.join(missing)}, which this machine "
            f"does not allow — so admitting it would grant them anyway, one hop "
            f"along. Allow them too, or withhold {command}")
    return True, ""

#: Who may be recorded as having pulled this, and why it is a closed set rather
#: than a string the caller fills in. `--via` goes into a claim note and a board
#: post, which is where an operator looks to answer the only question a session
#: that appeared on its own raises: WHAT STARTED THIS. A free-text field there is
#: one that can be made to say a human did — and the answer has to be worth more
#: than the thing it is explaining. Every trigger is a caller somebody wrote and
#: can go and read, so adding one is a line here and a line in `harness/README.md`.
#:
#: The default is `cli`, because a person at a prompt is the only caller that
#: needs no explaining, and it is the one this shipped with.
#:
#: `watch` is not a peer of the other two and should not be read as one. `cli`
#: and `dash` both have a human at the far end — a prompt somebody typed, a
#: button somebody clicked — and the provenance question they answer is only
#: *which* human. `watch` has none: it is the issue watcher acting on text it
#: read, and it is the first trigger here where the answer to "what started
#: this" is "nobody, a timer did". That is the whole of what #63 was nervous
#: about, so it is worth being able to grep the board for it by name.
TRIGGERS = {
    "cli": "a person typed it",
    "dash": "the ⚒ on qb-dash-tui, on a click somebody made",
    "watch": "the issue watcher, on an issue it found actionable (#63)",
}
DEFAULT_VIA = "cli"

#: Where a machine says yes, and there is NO environment override for it.
#:
#: There was one while this was being written — a `$QUARTERBACK_SPAWN` naming a
#: file, for the tests and for a human trying it out — and the codex review was
#: right that it falsified the sentence above it: an agent that can set an
#: environment variable could point the gate at a policy file inside the
#: repository it is working in, which is precisely the party the gate exists to
#: exclude. Removed rather than narrowed, because a permission with a convenient
#: bypass is not one.
#:
#: `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` still moves it, codex raised that too, and it is ANSWERED
#: rather than fixed — because it is not the same kind of hole. `$QUARTERBACK_SPAWN`
#: existed only for this gate: setting it was a pure escalation implying no other
#: capability. `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is where `qbdata.resolve_config` finds
#: `quarterback/config`, whose `QUARTERBACK_TOKEN_CMD` is a shell command this
#: fleet runs on every board call — so anyone who can set it for a harness process
#: already has arbitrary execution as the user, and could equally shadow `claude`
#: on `$PATH` or simply run it. A permission gate is not an environment sandbox
#: and cannot be made into one from inside a script the environment starts. What
#: it does stop is the thing it is for: REPOSITORY CONTENT — an issue body, a
#: CLAUDE.md, a committed file — being what grants the permission, because nothing
#: here reads a file the repository controls. `policy_path` returning None rather
#: than a relative path is the same property, held against the case where a
#: variable is missing instead of set.
#:
#: The rest of the resolution: `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` still moves it, and that is not the same
#: hole: it is the standard resolution `qb-env`, `qb-seat` and `qbdata` already
#: use, so anyone who controls it already controls the board token this would be
#: spent on. The tests point it at a temporary directory, which exercises the
#: real resolution rather than an override that only exists for them.
POLICY_NAME = "quarterback/spawn.json"

#: How many spawns may be live on this box at once when the policy names no
#: number. One, not none and not many: a policy file that has been written
#: deliberately means to spawn something, and the number that cannot surprise
#: anybody is the smallest one that works.
DEFAULT_MAX_SESSIONS = 1

#: The pane options a spawn wears. `@qb_spawn` says a pane is one of ours;
#: `@qb_spawn_ended` is written BY THE PANE ITSELF when the agent exits, which is
#: what keeps the cap honest — the window outlives the agent on purpose (its
#: output is the only record of what happened), so counting windows would fill
#: the cap with corpses and counting `pane_current_command` would be guesswork
#: about which process a shell is.
OPT_SPAWN = "@qb_spawn"
OPT_ENDED = "@qb_spawn_ended"
OPT_SESSION = "@qb_session"
OPT_LABEL = "@qb_label"
#: And what pulled it, on the pane itself — see `--via`. Stamped best-effort and
#: deliberately outside the checked three below: the other options are what make
#: a spawn countable and endable, and a window that lost one of those is closed
#: rather than run. Provenance is not that. A pane whose `@qb_spawn_via` did not
#: land is still counted, still claimed and still endable, and the board post
#: says who asked for it either way — so killing a working session over a label
#: would trade the thing that matters for the thing that explains it.
OPT_VIA = "@qb_spawn_via"


# ---- the machine's policy ----------------------------------------------------

class Policy:
    """What this machine will start, or why it will start nothing.

    Never raises, and every failure to read one is the same answer: no. See the
    module docstring on why this fails closed where `qb-admit` fails open.
    """

    def __init__(self, enabled: bool, commands: frozenset[str], max_sessions: int,
                 skip_permissions: bool, where: str, refusal: str) -> None:
        self.enabled = enabled
        self.commands = commands
        self.max_sessions = max_sessions
        self.skip_permissions = skip_permissions
        self.where = where
        self.refusal = refusal


def policy_path() -> Path | None:
    """The one file that can say yes, or None when there is no config home at all.

    **None, and not a relative path.** `os.path.join("", ".config")` is
    `.config` — so with neither `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` nor `$HOME` set, the obvious
    spelling of this resolves the gate against the CURRENT DIRECTORY, and a
    repository shipping `.config/quarterback/spawn.json` would then be granting
    itself the permission. That is the exact party this exists to exclude,
    reached by a variable being absent rather than by one being set. A machine
    with no config home has not opted in to anything.

    **And None rather than `expanduser("~")`, which is what every neighbour does
    here** — `qbdata.resolve_config`, `mcp.board.config.config_path` (whose
    comment names this same trap) and `harness_rules` all fall back to the passwd
    entry, and they are right to. The difference is what the file IS. Theirs
    answer "where is my board token", where failing to find it means the tool
    cannot work, so reaching past a missing `$HOME` to the real home is the
    helpful answer. This one answers "may I start an agent", where failing to
    find it means the tool must not ACT — and "I cannot name a config home" is a
    perfectly good no. In practice nothing reaches it: a user systemd unit sets
    `$HOME`, and so does every shell.
    """
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or (
        os.path.join(home, ".config") if (home := os.environ.get("HOME")) else "")
    if not base or not os.path.isabs(base):
        return None
    return Path(base) / POLICY_NAME


def read_policy() -> Policy:
    path = policy_path()

    def no(why: str) -> Policy:
        return Policy(False, frozenset(), 0, False, str(path or "(nowhere)"), why)

    if path is None:
        return no("neither $XDG_CONFIG_HOME nor $HOME names a config directory, so "
                  "there is nowhere this machine could have said yes — and a "
                  "relative path here would let the checkout being worked in "
                  "grant the permission to itself")
    if not path.is_file():
        return no(f"spawning is not enabled on this machine — there is no {path}. "
                  f"A machine opts in by setting "
                  f"`programs.quarterback-harness.spawn.enable = true` and "
                  f"naming the commands it will start; nothing a repository or an "
                  f"agent can write turns this on")
    try:
        raw = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except Exception as e:                                        # noqa: BLE001
        return no(f"{path} would not parse ({e}) — refusing. A permission that "
                  f"cannot be read is not a permission")
    if not isinstance(raw, dict):
        return no(f"{path} is a {type(raw).__name__}, not an object — refusing")
    if raw.get("enabled") is not True:
        return no(f"{path} does not say `\"enabled\": true` — refusing. The key is "
                  f"compared to the literal true, so a string, a 1 and an absence "
                  f"are all no")

    commands = raw.get("commands")
    if commands is None:
        commands = []
    if not isinstance(commands, list) or any(not isinstance(c, str) for c in commands):
        return no(f"`commands` in {path} is not a list of strings — refusing, "
                  f"because a spawn allowlist that cannot be read is not one")

    ceiling = raw.get("max_sessions", DEFAULT_MAX_SESSIONS)
    if ceiling is None:
        ceiling = DEFAULT_MAX_SESSIONS
    if bad_ceiling(ceiling):
        return no(f"`max_sessions` in {path} is {ceiling!r} — it takes a "
                  f"non-negative integer (0 is a freeze). Refusing")

    skip = raw.get("skip_permissions", True)
    if not isinstance(skip, bool):
        return no(f"`skip_permissions` in {path} is {skip!r}, not a boolean — refusing")

    return Policy(True, frozenset(commands), ceiling, skip, str(path), "")


# ---- the board's dials -------------------------------------------------------

#: This machine's ceiling, when the board states one. The file's number is the
#: fallback and stays exactly what it was; see the module docstring on why this one
#: key of the three moved and the other two did not.
DIAL_MACHINE = "spawn.max_sessions"

#: And the fleet's, which has no file to fall back to because a per-machine file
#: cannot express a fleet-wide number. Unset is NO CEILING, which is what the fleet
#: ran under before this existed.
DIAL_FLEET = "spawn.max_sessions_fleet"

#: How long the dial read waits. `harness_rules.DIALS_TIMEOUT`'s number and its
#: argument: this runs on every spawn AND on every `--policy`, which a dashboard
#: asks on every click, so a board that is down must cost a moment rather than half
#: a minute. The answer when it lapses is the policy file's number, which is a
#: usable answer; thirty seconds of nothing is not.
DIALS_TIMEOUT = 5

#: And how long the fleet COUNT waits. Longer, because it is asked far less often —
#: only when a fleet ceiling is actually set, and only after every cheaper refusal
#: above it has passed — and because being wrong here costs a spawn that should have
#: been refused rather than a dashboard that hangs.
ACTIVE_TIMEOUT = 10


def bad_ceiling(value: object) -> bool:
    """Is this not a number of sessions? ONE rule, over the file and the board both.

    `bool` is an `int` in python and `true` is a plausible thing to type into a key
    next to `enabled` — or into a value box on a dashboard — so it is refused rather
    than counted as 1, the rule `qb-admit` applies to its own ceiling.

    Shared rather than written twice, because the two sources now say the same thing
    in different places: a ceiling the file would refuse and the board would accept
    is a ceiling whose meaning depends on which layer carried it, which is the exact
    failure the dial layer's own `_dial_problem` exists to prevent one level up. The
    board cannot make this check for us — `POST /dials` stores an opaque JSON value
    on purpose — and `harness_rules.dial_problem` checks only that it is a number,
    so `-1` reaches here having passed everything in front of it.
    """
    return isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int) or value < 0


class Ceilings(NamedTuple):
    """What the board says about how hard this box, and this fleet, may work.

    `None` on either is "the board did not say", never "zero" — and the distinction
    is the whole of why this is a NamedTuple and not two ints. Zero is a freeze, a
    decision somebody made and typed a reason for; None is the ordinary state of a
    fleet that has never set one, and it is also what an outage looks like. The
    first must refuse every spawn and the second must refuse none, so a layer that
    collapsed them would turn a board hiccup into a fleet-wide stop.

    `problem` is why there is no answer, and it is REPORTED rather than acted on:
    every gate this feeds fails open. It is not empty on the ordinary path — a box
    on no board at all resolves no config and says so — which is why nothing prints
    it unless a ceiling was actually wanted.
    """

    machine: int | None
    fleet: int | None
    problem: str


def read_ceilings(repo_path: str | None = None) -> Ceilings:
    """Ask the board for both ceilings. Never raises; every failure is `problem`.

    **ONLY THE FLEET SCOPE ANSWERS, and a repo-scoped row is NAMED rather than
    dropped.** A machine's concurrency is not a property of a repository: with
    `acme/widget` at 5 and `acme/gadget` at 2 and three live spawns across both,
    there is no question `live_spawns()` has answered — it counts panes on this tmux
    server and does not know which checkout each one is in. So the scope is asked for
    with the repo (which is why this takes one at all) purely so that a row written
    at the wrong scope can be reported. `harness_rules.dial_scope_problem` refuses
    that write in the dashboard's picker, but the board takes either scope from
    anything holding a human key — including a `curl` and the web page, which have
    no dial vocabulary and by design cannot have one. A setting stored, reported as
    in force and read by nothing is the exact failure this whole layer exists to end,
    and the reader is the last place it can still be said out loud.

    **A value this refuses is not a value it substitutes.** A malformed dial leaves
    the ceiling where it was — on the policy file — and says so, rather than falling
    to a compiled-in number that neither layer asked for.
    """
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
        from qbdata import board_client, repo_slug
        client, _cfg = board_client()
        # Best-effort and never fatal: with no slug this asks for the fleet rows
        # alone, which is the answer either way. The repo buys the REPORT, not the
        # ceiling, so a checkout that cannot name itself loses a diagnostic rather
        # than a number.
        slug = None
        if repo_path:
            try:
                slug = repo_slug(repo_path)
            except Exception:                                     # noqa: BLE001
                slug = None
        body = client.get("/dials", {"repo": slug} if slug else None,
                          timeout=DIALS_TIMEOUT)
    except Exception as e:                                        # noqa: BLE001
        return Ceilings(None, None, f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}")
    # `isinstance`, not `body or {}`. A JSON ARRAY is truthy, and `[].get` is an
    # AttributeError raised OUTSIDE the try above — so the one shape this layer
    # exists to survive, a board answering something unexpected, would crash the
    # spawner instead of leaving the file's ceiling in force. `_dial_body` makes the
    # same check in the same words one directory over.
    if not isinstance(body, dict):
        return Ceilings(None, None,
                        f"the board answered a {type(body).__name__}, not an object")
    rows = body.get("dials")
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        return Ceilings(None, None, "the board's `dials` was not a list")
    found: dict[str, int] = {}
    problems: list[str] = []
    for row in rows:
        if not isinstance(row, dict):
            continue
        name = row.get("dial")
        if name not in (DIAL_MACHINE, DIAL_FLEET):
            continue
        if row.get("repo"):
            problems.append(
                f"`{name}` is set on the board for {row['repo']} — ignored. It is "
                f"fleet configuration: this ceiling counts panes on one tmux server "
                f"and does not know which checkout each is in, so a per-repo answer "
                f"is not one. Set it with no repo")
            continue
        # AN AGENT MAY NOT RAISE ITS OWN CEILING, and since #591 that has to be
        # enforced HERE rather than assumed from the gate. `POST /dials` used to
        # take `app.auth.human`, which is what made this dial safe to put on the
        # board at all — see the module docstring. It now also accepts an agent
        # holding its machine's delegated secret, so the board alone no longer
        # answers "was a person behind this".
        #
        # It still records the answer, which is the whole reason `set_via` exists:
        # `agent` means no person was in the request. This is the ONE consumer that
        # has to care, because it is the one dial whose subject is the agent
        # reading it — a self-approval shape #85, #86, #78, #232 and #335 each
        # settled separately, and reopening it by a longer route would be exactly
        # the failure #479 names.
        #
        # Refused rather than clamped, and noisily: a spawner that quietly used the
        # file's number would leave somebody looking at a board that says 40 and a
        # box behaving like 8, with nothing connecting the two.
        #
        # `None` is HONOURED. Null is "not recorded" — a row written before the
        # column existed, which is to say before an agent could write one at all —
        # and treating it as suspect would retire ceilings nobody can see are being
        # ignored. Only the explicit `agent` is refused.
        via = row.get("set_via")
        # AN ALLOWLIST, not `!= "agent"`. The three values below are the ways a
        # PERSON proves it (`app.auth`); anything else is refused, so a misspelling,
        # a value from a newer board than this box, or a malformed row cannot become
        # trusted provenance merely by failing to match one string. A denylist here
        # answers "is this the one bad value I know about" when the question is "do
        # I know this was a person".
        #
        # `None` is honoured, and it is the one exception. Null means the row
        # predates the column — and every return path through `human()` and
        # `delegated()` sets a method, so a row written today cannot be null.
        # Refusing it would silently retire ceilings set before the column existed,
        # which is the failure this whole function is trying not to have.
        if via is not None and via not in ("edge", "key", "dev"):
            problems.append(
                f"`{name}` on the board was set by {row.get('set_by') or 'an agent'} "
                f"({via!r}), which is not a person — ignored. This is the one dial an "
                f"agent may not set for itself: it is the ceiling on the agent reading "
                f"it. Ask a person to set it, from a browser or with an X-Human-Key")
            continue
        value = row.get("value")
        if bad_ceiling(value):
            problems.append(f"`{name}` on the board is {value!r}, which is not a "
                            f"non-negative whole number of sessions — ignored")
            continue
        found[name] = value
    return Ceilings(found.get(DIAL_MACHINE), found.get(DIAL_FLEET),
                    "; ".join(problems))


def ceiling_from(ceilings: Ceilings) -> str:
    """The `(from …)` half of a ceiling line, or nothing at all.

    Empty when the file answered, because that is the state every message in this
    file was written against and naming it on every line would be noise. Said out
    loud when the BOARD answered, because a number that is not the one in the
    operator's nix config is a number they will otherwise go looking for in the
    wrong file.
    """
    return "" if ceilings.machine is None else f" (from the board's `{DIAL_MACHINE}`)"


def live_agents() -> tuple[int | None, str]:
    """How many agents are live across the whole board. `(count, why not)`.

    `None` is "could not tell", and unlike :func:`live_spawns` it is not a refusal —
    see the module docstring. The local ceiling has already been applied by the time
    anything calls this, so a board that will not answer degrades the fleet to its
    per-machine bound rather than to nothing.

    **Top-level agents only.** `GET /active` returns `subagents` beside them and
    they are deliberately not counted: a fan-out is one session's own tooling, it
    holds no pane and no seat, and counting it would make a ceiling expressed in
    sessions bite on something that is not one.
    """
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
        from qbdata import board_client
        client, _cfg = board_client()
        body = client.get("/active", timeout=ACTIVE_TIMEOUT)
    except Exception as e:                                        # noqa: BLE001
        return None, f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}"
    # Checked rather than assumed, for `read_ceilings`' reason: outside the try, a
    # `.get` on a list is an AttributeError, and this gate's whole promise is that a
    # board it cannot read leaves the local ceiling in charge rather than stopping
    # the spawn.
    if not isinstance(body, dict):
        return None, f"the board answered a {type(body).__name__}, not an object"
    agents = body.get("agents")
    if not isinstance(agents, list):
        return None, "the board's `agents` was not a list"
    return len(agents), ""


# ---- the pane ---------------------------------------------------------------

def tmux(*args: str, timeout: int = 10) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """One tmux call, never raising. A box with no tmux is a state, not a crash."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["tmux", *args], capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=timeout)
    except Exception as e:                                        # noqa: BLE001
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(args, 127, "", f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}")


def live_spawns() -> list[dict] | None:
    """Spawned panes on this tmux server whose agent has not exited, or None.

    `list-panes -a` is the whole server rather than one screen, which is what the
    cap is about: the limit is on the MACHINE, so a second screen must not get a
    second allowance.

    **None is "cannot tell", and it is not []** — the distinction the codex review
    found missing. A `list-panes` that fails read as zero live spawns, which is a
    cap that switches itself off exactly when the thing it counts has become
    unreadable. The caller refuses on it.

    No tmux at all IS zero, and honestly so: a spawn is a tmux window, so a box
    with no multiplexer is running none. It refuses a few steps later for having
    nowhere to put one.
    """
    if not os.environ.get("TMUX"):
        return []
    fmt = "\t".join("#{%s}" % f for f in
                    ("pane_id", OPT_SPAWN, OPT_ENDED, OPT_SESSION, "pane_dead"))
    got = tmux("list-panes", "-a", "-F", fmt, timeout=5)
    if got.returncode != 0:
        return None
    live = []
    for line in got.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = line.split("\t")
        if len(parts) != 5 or not parts[1]:
            continue
        if parts[2] or parts[4] == "1":
            continue
        live.append({"pane": parts[0], "command": parts[1], "session": parts[3]})
    return live


# ---- the neighbours ----------------------------------------------------------

def sibling(name: str) -> str:
    """`name` on PATH, or beside this script. The resolution `qb-seat.pace_cmd`
    makes, for the reason it gives: a home-manager install has both, a checkout
    has only the second, and a partial install has only the first."""
    from shutil import which
    return which(name) or str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent / name)


def run_gate(name: str, *args: str, timeout: int = 60) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """A neighbouring qb- tool, its stderr passed straight through.

    NOT captured: `qb-claim` naming the holder and `qb-admit` listing the slots
    are the whole use of a refusal, and a spawner that swallowed them would refuse
    with less to say than the tools it called.
    """
    try:
        return subprocess.run([sibling(name), *args], text=True, timeout=timeout,
                              stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except Exception as e:                                        # noqa: BLE001
        # Said out loud, not only returned. The caller turns any non-zero into a
        # refusal naming the exit code, and "127" on its own sends an operator
        # looking for a gate that answered rather than one that never ran — the
        # difference between a missing install and a 30-second timeout is the
        # whole of what they need.
        print(f"qb-start: {name} did not run — {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess((name,), 127, None, f"{e}")


def board_post(kind: str, summary: str, detail: str, refs: list[dict]) -> None:
    """Tell the board, best-effort and in silence.

    Every spawn is a post before it is a process, and a refusal is posted too —
    that is what makes a fleet's spawning readable rather than something you find
    out about by noticing a pane. It is best-effort for the reason `qb-stage` and
    the seat's name registration are: a session that refused to start because a
    record of it could not be written would cost more than the record is worth,
    and the pane is the durable evidence either way.
    """
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
        from qbdata import board_client
        client, _cfg = board_client()
        client.post("/post", {"type": kind, "summary": summary[:400],
                              "detail": detail, "refs": refs})
    except Exception:                                             # noqa: BLE001
        pass


# ---- the command -------------------------------------------------------------

def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="qb-start", description=__doc__,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("command", nargs="?",
                    help="the slash command to start — one of: "
                         + ", ".join(sorted(SPAWNABLE)))
    ap.add_argument("number", nargs="?",
                    help="the issue or PR number it takes")
    ap.add_argument("--repo-path", default=".",
                    help="the checkout the session runs in (default: here)")
    ap.add_argument("--model", default=None,
                    help="passed to the agent as --model; omitted when unset")
    ap.add_argument("--via", default=DEFAULT_VIA, choices=sorted(TRIGGERS),
                    help="what pulled this, recorded on the claim, the board post "
                         "and the pane, so a session that appeared can be traced "
                         "to the thing that asked for it (default: "
                         + DEFAULT_VIA + ")")
    ap.add_argument("--policy", action="store_true",
                    help="say what this machine will start, and exit. Starts "
                         "nothing, claims nothing and posts nothing — it is the "
                         "question a caller asks before it offers somebody a "
                         "button. On a machine that has opted in it also reads the "
                         "board's ceiling, bounded and failing open to the policy "
                         "file, because a freeze is exactly what such a caller "
                         "must see; a machine that has not opted in reaches "
                         "nothing but its own config directory. Exits 0 when "
                         "spawning is enabled here, 3 when it is not")
    ap.add_argument("--no-board", action="store_true",
                    help="with --policy: answer from the policy file alone, "
                         "consulting no board at all. For a caller that asks this "
                         "on a UI thread, where a board that is down would cost it "
                         "five seconds of frozen screen per keystroke. The ceiling "
                         "reported is then the FILE's, `max_sessions_source` says "
                         "so, and the spawn path still applies the dial")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                    help="make every refusal, print what would run, start nothing "
                         "— and take no claim and post nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true",
                    help="print the whole answer on stdout")
    ap.add_argument("--quiet", "-q", action="store_true",
                    help="exit code only — nothing on stdout or stderr, and it wins "
                         "over --json, because that is the stronger promise")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    def say(message: str) -> None:
        if not args.quiet:
            print(message, file=sys.stderr)

    def out(**fields) -> None:
        if args.json and not args.quiet:
            print(json.dumps(fields, indent=2))

    if args.no_board and not args.policy:
        # Refused rather than ignored, and through `say` so `--quiet` still means
        # exit code only. On the spawn path the dial is what decides whether this
        # box may start anything at all, so a flag that appeared to switch it off
        # would be a caller believing it had opted out of a ceiling it is still
        # subject to — and the one thing a gate must never do is look like it took
        # an instruction it did not.
        say("qb-start: --no-board answers --policy and nothing else. A spawn "
            "applies the board's ceiling; there is no opting out of it from the "
            "command line")
        return MISUSE

    # ---- 1. the machine. FIRST, and before anything is imported, looked for or
    # asked. This is the default path and it has to cost nothing at all.
    policy = read_policy()

    # ---- 1a. …and a caller may ask that same question without asking for a
    # session. A trigger with a button on it needs the answer BEFORE the click:
    # an affordance that looks live, is clicked, and then explains that this
    # machine never opted in has spent somebody's attention to tell them
    # something it knew all along. The alternative is for the caller to read
    # `spawn.json` itself, which is a second implementation of the one gate this
    # file exists to be the only copy of.
    #
    # It is the SAME `read_policy` the spawn path uses and it happens at the same
    # point, so it can never answer yes to a machine the next line would refuse.
    # It consults no tmux and takes no claim, and the ONE thing it does leave the
    # box for is the ceiling — bounded at `DIALS_TIMEOUT` and failing open to the
    # file, on a machine that has already said yes. See the block below for why
    # that is worth a caller's five seconds and why the not-enabled path still
    # touches nothing at all.
    if args.policy:
        # The one thing `--policy` now leaves this box for, and the paragraph it
        # edits is its own: this used to consult "nothing but the policy file",
        # which was true and stopped being the right promise the moment the ceiling
        # could be frozen from the board (#563). A caller asks this BEFORE it offers
        # somebody a button, and `spawn.max_sessions: 0` is exactly the answer such
        # a caller must have — a button that looks live, takes the click and then
        # explains that the fleet is frozen has spent somebody's attention telling
        # them something it could have known. The cost is one bounded GET that fails
        # open to the file, on a machine that has already said yes; a box that never
        # opted in still reaches none of it, which is the property that mattered.
        ceilings = (read_ceilings(args.repo_path)
                    if policy.enabled and not args.no_board
                    else Ceilings(None, None, ""))
        ceiling = policy.max_sessions if ceilings.machine is None else ceilings.machine
        allows = sorted(c for c in policy.commands & set(SPAWNABLE)
                        if admits(c, policy.commands)[0])
        # Named separately rather than silently dropped: a command the
        # policy lists and this refuses is the operator's own file
        # disagreeing with itself, and the one thing they cannot see
        # from the file alone (#541).
        withheld = sorted(set(policy.commands & set(SPAWNABLE)) - set(allows))
        if policy.enabled:
            say(f"spawning is enabled here — {policy.where}")
            say(f"  allows: {', '.join(allows) or '(nothing — `commands` is empty)'}"
                f"; up to {ceiling} at once{ceiling_from(ceilings)}")
            if ceilings.fleet is not None:
                say(f"  and up to {ceilings.fleet} live across the whole board "
                    f"(`{DIAL_FLEET}`)")
            if ceilings.problem:
                say(f"  the board's ceiling was not used: {ceilings.problem}")
            for c in withheld:
                say(f"  listed but refused: {admits(c, policy.commands)[1]}")
        else:
            say(f"not enabled: {policy.refusal}")
        # `max_sessions` stays the EFFECTIVE ceiling and does not change meaning:
        # every caller reading it is asking "how many will this box run", and the
        # answer to that is the dial when there is one. `max_sessions_policy` is
        # the fallback underneath, named separately so a caller can tell a box
        # that was configured at 2 from a fleet that was dialled to 2.
        out(enabled=policy.enabled, policy=policy.where, commands=allows,
            withheld=withheld,
            max_sessions=ceiling, max_sessions_policy=policy.max_sessions,
            max_sessions_source="board" if ceilings.machine is not None else "policy",
            max_sessions_fleet=ceilings.fleet,
            dials_problem=ceilings.problem or None,
            reason=policy.refusal or None,
            spawnable=sorted(SPAWNABLE), triggers=sorted(TRIGGERS))
        return STARTED if policy.enabled else NOT_ENABLED

    if not policy.enabled:
        say(f"not enabled: {policy.refusal}")
        out(started=False, enabled=False, policy=policy.where, reason=policy.refusal)
        # Not posted. On a machine that has not opted in there is nothing to
        # report — nobody asked the board for this — and a refusal that phoned
        # home would break the property that the default touches nothing.
        return NOT_ENABLED

    if not args.command:
        say("qb-start: name a command and its number, e.g. `qb-start /fix-issue 277`")
        say(f"  this machine allows: {', '.join(sorted(policy.commands)) or '(none)'}")
        return MISUSE

    command = args.command if args.command.startswith("/") else "/" + args.command
    repo_path = os.path.abspath(args.repo_path)

    # What asked for this, in the words the board post carries. On the refusals
    # as well as the spawns: "the dash was refused forty times today because this
    # machine never opted in" is a sentence somebody should be able to read off
    # the board, and a refusal that did not say who was refused cannot say it.
    def provenance(*parts: str) -> str:
        return "\n\n".join([*(p for p in parts if p),
                             f"asked for by: {args.via} — {TRIGGERS[args.via]}"])

    def refuse(code: int, summary: str, detail: str = "") -> int:
        say(f"refused: {summary}")
        if detail:
            say(f"  {detail}")
        out(started=False, enabled=True, policy=policy.where, command=command,
            via=args.via, reason=summary)
        if not args.dry_run:
            board_post("note", f"spawn refused: {summary}",
                       provenance(detail), board_refs(repo_path, command, args.number))
        return code

    # ---- 2. the allowlist, both halves. The table is what CAN be spawned; the
    # policy is what this machine WILL spawn, and it can only take things away.
    if command not in SPAWNABLE:
        return refuse(NOT_ALLOWED,
                      f"{command} is not a spawnable command",
                      "the set is compiled in so that a policy file cannot widen "
                      "it: " + ", ".join(sorted(SPAWNABLE)))
    ok, why = admits(command, policy.commands)
    if not ok:
        allowed = ", ".join(sorted(policy.commands)) or (
            "nothing — `commands` is empty, which is the second lock: turning "
            "spawning on is one decision and saying what may come through it is "
            "another")
        return refuse(NOT_ALLOWED, why, f"{policy.where} allows: {allowed}")

    # ---- 3. the argument. One positive integer, because that is the whole of
    # what these commands take and anything else is the free text being refused.
    #
    # ASCII, and matched rather than tested with `isdigit`. `"٧".isdigit()` is
    # True and `int("٧")` is 7, so a naive check hands `/fix-issue` an issue
    # number nobody typed and nothing downstream would recognise; `"½".isdigit()`
    # is False but `isnumeric` says otherwise, which is the same trap one method
    # along. The set of characters an issue number may contain is ten of them.
    #
    # Both directions, because the arity is now a property of the command rather
    # than an assumption about the table. A numberless command handed a number is
    # as much a misuse as a numbered one without: it means the caller believes it
    # is starting work on something specific, and it is not.
    kind = SPAWNABLE[command].kind
    if kind is None:
        if args.number is not None:
            say(f"qb-start: {command} takes no number — got {args.number!r}. It "
                f"reads the plan and selects its own item, so a number here is a "
                f"caller believing it aimed this at something. It did not")
            return MISUSE
        number = None
    else:
        if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+", args.number or "") or int(args.number) <= 0:
            say(f"qb-start: {command} takes an issue or PR number — got "
                f"{args.number!r}. The brief is a named command with arguments, not "
                f"free text, so nothing else is passed through")
            return MISUSE
        number = str(int(args.number))

    # ---- 4. the checkout.
    if not os.path.isdir(repo_path):
        say(f"qb-start: {repo_path} is not a directory")
        return MISUSE
    inside = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", repo_path, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
                            capture_output=True, text=True)
    if inside.returncode != 0:
        say(f"qb-start: {repo_path} is not a git worktree — a session starts in one, "
            f"and the claim is keyed on its origin remote")
        return MISUSE

    # ---- 5. this machine's own ceiling.
    #
    # The DIAL first and the file behind it (#563). Asked here rather than beside
    # `read_policy` so that a misuse — an unknown command, a missing number, a path
    # that is not a checkout — still costs no board call: every refusal above this
    # line is answerable from the file, and this is the first point at which the
    # number is actually needed.
    ceilings = read_ceilings(repo_path)
    ceiling = policy.max_sessions if ceilings.machine is None else ceilings.machine
    if ceilings.problem:
        # Said, not acted on. A dial that could not be read leaves the file's number
        # in force — the trade `qb-admit` makes for its own restriction — and an
        # operator who set one is the only person who can tell whether that is the
        # number they wanted.
        say(f"qb-start: the board's ceiling was not used ({ceilings.problem})")
        if ceilings.machine is None:
            # Only when the FILE is what ended up answering. A refused repo-scoped
            # row beside a good fleet one is worth reporting and is not a fallback,
            # and saying "the file says 2" over a board ceiling of 5 would name the
            # wrong number as the one in force.
            say(f"  the ceiling is {policy.where}'s {policy.max_sessions}")
    running = live_spawns()
    if running is None:
        return refuse(AT_CAP, "this machine's spawned panes could not be counted",
                      "tmux is here and would not list its panes, so the ceiling "
                      "cannot be applied — and a cap that switches itself off when "
                      "its input goes unreadable is not one")
    if len(running) >= ceiling:
        # The remedy names the layer that actually decided, because "raise
        # max_sessions in ~/.config/quarterback/spawn.json" sends somebody to edit a
        # file whose number is being overridden — and on this fleet that edit is a
        # nix build, a PR and a rebuild before they find out it changed nothing.
        where = (f"clear or raise `{DIAL_MACHINE}` on the board"
                 if ceilings.machine is not None
                 else f"raise `max_sessions` in {policy.where}")
        return refuse(AT_CAP,
                      f"{len(running)} of {ceiling} spawned session(s) "
                      f"are already running on this machine",
                      f"close one, or {where}"
                      + (" (0 is a freeze)" if ceiling == 0 else ""))

    # ---- 5b. the FLEET's ceiling. Only when one is set, and it fails OPEN — the
    # module docstring has both arguments at length. In one line each: unset is how
    # this fleet ran before the dial existed, and a gate that refused on an
    # unreachable board would stop every box over a hiccup while the per-machine
    # ceiling underneath is what actually bounds a runaway.
    if ceilings.fleet is not None:
        live, why_not = live_agents()
        if live is None:
            say(f"qb-start: could not count the fleet's live agents ({why_not}) — "
                f"starting anyway, because this machine's own ceiling of {ceiling} "
                f"is what bounds a runaway and it has already been applied")
        elif live >= ceilings.fleet:
            return refuse(AT_FLEET_CAP,
                          f"{live} of {ceilings.fleet} agent(s) are live across the "
                          f"whole board",
                          "nothing on this box moves that — wait for a session to "
                          "end, or raise `" + DIAL_FLEET + "` on the board. It "
                          "counts every live agent and not only spawned ones, so "
                          "seats somebody opened by hand are in that number")

    # ---- 6 and 7. THE GATES, AND ONE RULE OVER BOTH: a spawn proceeds only on a
    # definite go. Not "anything that is not a refusal" — an outage, a malformed
    # ceiling, a `qb-pace` that could not read the caps and a tool missing from a
    # partial install all reach this as some other number, and every one of them
    # means the gate did not run rather than that it passed.
    #
    # That is the OPPOSITE of what `create-worktree` does with the same two
    # answers, and the asymmetry is the whole point rather than an oversight. A
    # checkout failing open is a human who has already decided to work being told
    # the board is unreachable; a spawn failing open is an unattended session
    # nobody decided on, against a ceiling nobody could read. #244's rule applied
    # to the risky direction: a governor that cannot read its input must not
    # report clear.
    #
    # It costs a human one retry, and only in the cases where something is
    # already broken. `qb-pace` and `qb-admit` ship in the same package as this
    # file and `sibling()` falls back to the directory beside it, so "not
    # installed" is not a state a real install reaches.
    pace = run_gate("qb-pace", "--gate", timeout=30)
    if pace.returncode == 3:
        return refuse(PACED, "the shared subscription's window is spent",
                      "it comes back — qb-pace's line above says when")
    if pace.returncode != 0:
        return refuse(PACED, "how the shared subscription stands could not be read",
                      f"qb-pace exited {pace.returncode} (4 is 'the caps could not "
                      f"be obtained at all'). An API-key install with no caps to "
                      f"report answers `go`, so this is not that")

    admit = run_gate("qb-admit", "--repo-path", repo_path, timeout=30)
    if admit.returncode == 1:
        return refuse(FULL, f"{os.path.basename(repo_path)}'s in-flight window is full")
    if admit.returncode != 0:
        return refuse(FULL, "how much of this repo is in flight could not be read",
                      f"qb-admit exited {admit.returncode} — a board it could not "
                      f"reach, or a ceiling it would not act on. An unbounded repo "
                      f"answers `room` without asking anything, so this is not that")

    # ---- 7b. is there anything to get involved WITH? Numberless commands only.
    #
    # This edits the not-a-dispatcher paragraph at the top of this file rather
    # than quietly slipping past it, so it is stated here too (#541): asking
    # whether the plan has ANYTHING free is not picking what to take. Nothing is
    # passed to the agent, and the item it eventually claims may not be the one
    # that was free at this moment. It is the question `qb-admit` already asks —
    # is there room — with no say in what goes in the space.
    #
    # What it buys is the refusal that costs nothing. Without a pre-spawn claim,
    # `/get-involved` against a fully-claimed plan starts a session that reads the
    # plan, finds nothing, reports it and stops: correct, and a whole session
    # spent to reach it. Harmless once; at the tail of a drain it is the common
    # case, and it is the case an eager fleet hits repeatedly.
    #
    # Fails OPEN, unlike the gates above it. A board that cannot answer "is
    # anything free" has told us nothing about the plan, and refusing on silence
    # would make an unreachable board look like a finished one — which is the
    # failure this whole fleet spent a night on. The gates that fail closed are
    # the ones counting a resource; this one is avoiding a wasted session.
    if kind is None and not args.dry_run:
        free, why_not = plan_has_free_item(repo_path)
        if free is False:
            return refuse(HELD,
                          "nothing on the plan is free to pick up",
                          "every open item is claimed, blocked or held. This is the "
                          "refusal that costs nothing: without it the session would "
                          "start, read the plan, find the same thing and stop. "
                          "`plan_read` shows who holds what")
        if free is None:
            say(f"qb-start: could not ask whether the plan has anything free "
                f"({why_not}) — starting anyway, because a board that did not "
                f"answer has said nothing about the plan")

    if args.dry_run:
        session = "<a fresh uuid4>"
        say(f"policy:   {policy.where}")
        say(f"ceiling:  {len(running)} of {ceiling} on this machine"
            f"{ceiling_from(ceilings)}"
            + (f"; the fleet allows {ceilings.fleet}" if ceilings.fleet is not None
               else ""))
        say(f"cwd:      {repo_path}")
        if kind is None:
            say("claim:    none — this command selects and claims its own "
                "item, atomically, once it has read the plan")
        else:
            say(f"claim:    {kind} {number} (no session, machine-held, as "
                f"create-worktree's is)")
        say(f"via:      {args.via} — {TRIGGERS[args.via]}")
        say(f"session:  {session}")
        argv = agent_argv(command, number, policy, args.model, session)
        say(f"command:  {' '.join(shlex.quote(a) for a in argv)}")
        out(started=False, dry_run=True, enabled=True, policy=policy.where,
            command=command, number=number, via=args.via,
            claim=None if kind is None else {"kind": kind, "value": number},
            repo_path=repo_path, running=len(running), max_sessions=ceiling,
            max_sessions_policy=policy.max_sessions,
            max_sessions_source="board" if ceilings.machine is not None else "policy",
            max_sessions_fleet=ceilings.fleet)
        return STARTED

    # ---- 8. the claim, through the ordinary path, before the process exists.
    #
    # ONLY when the command names the work. `/get-involved` cannot take one here
    # because which item it will take is not known until it has read the plan, and
    # a claim on the wrong thing is worse than none (#541).
    #
    # The interlock is not lost, it moves inside the session: `plan_claim` is
    # atomic and is what makes three seats take three different items —
    # `get-involved.md` §2 already states the rule it enforces, "claims ONE item
    # BEFORE you start". A spawned `/get-involved` is the same session a human
    # runs, with nobody in the chair.
    #
    # And nothing goes uncounted, which is the property the claim was carrying two
    # jobs for. The SESSION count — `policy.max_sessions` over `live_spawns()`,
    # and `qb-admit`'s per-repo window — both applied above and neither needed a
    # claim to do it. What the claim adds is the WORK interlock, and that is the
    # only half being moved.
    session = str(uuid.uuid4())
    if kind is not None:
        claim = run_gate(
            "qb-claim", kind, number, "--repo-path", repo_path, "--session", "",
            "--note", f"spawned {command} {number} by qb-start via {args.via} "
                      f"(session {session[:8]})",
            "--quiet", timeout=60)
    else:
        claim = None
    if claim is not None and claim.returncode == 1:
        return refuse(HELD, f"{kind} {number} is already claimed",
                      "two agents on one unit of work is what the claim prevents — "
                      "run `qb-claimed` to see who has it")
    if claim is not None and claim.returncode != 0:
        # An outage is NOT proceeded through here, and that is the one place this
        # differs from `create-worktree`, deliberately. A checkout that cannot
        # reach the board is a human who has already decided to work; a spawn that
        # cannot reach the board is an unattended session nobody would know about,
        # taken out of a count nobody can see. The board being down is a reason
        # not to start something, and never a reason not to stop.
        return refuse(HELD, f"the claim on {kind} {number} could not be taken",
                      "qb-claim's own reason is above. A spawn is not started "
                      "uncounted: an unattended session that nothing can count is "
                      "worse than one nobody started")

    refs = board_refs(repo_path, command, number)
    what = command if number is None else f"{command} {number}"
    board_post("status", f"spawning {what} (via {args.via})",
               provenance(f"qb-start on this machine: session {session}, cwd "
                          f"{repo_path}, claim "
                          f"{'none — it claims its own item' if kind is None else f'{kind}/{number}'}"
                          f". Attachable in "
                          f"tmux; end it with `qb-end {session}`."), refs)

    # ---- 9. the pane.
    started, why = spawn(command, number, policy, args.model, session, repo_path,
                         args.via)
    if not started:
        # The claim goes back. It was taken for a session that now does not exist,
        # and leaving it would hold the slot the whole point of taking it was to
        # account for — for eight hours, over a window that never opened.
        #
        # And what the release ANSWERED is reported, rather than assumed. Saying
        # "handed back" over a release that failed is the worse half of two
        # failures: the slot is held AND the only record says it is free, so
        # nobody goes looking. The TTL is still underneath it, and eight hours is
        # exactly the wait this line exists to save somebody.
        # Nothing to hand back when nothing was taken (#541): the claimless path
        # simply does not reach here with a claim, and reporting "handed back"
        # over a claim that never existed would be the same lie as reporting it
        # over a release that failed.
        if kind is None:
            handed = None
            note = "no claim was taken — this command claims its own item, and it never got that far"
        else:
            gave_back = run_gate("qb-release", kind, number, "--repo-path", repo_path,
                                 "--session", "", "--quiet", timeout=30)
            handed = gave_back.returncode == 0
            note = (f"the claim on {kind} {number} was handed back" if handed else
                    f"the claim on {kind} {number} could NOT be handed back "
                    f"(qb-release exited {gave_back.returncode}) — it holds a slot "
                    f"until its TTL runs out, or until `qb-release {kind} {number}`")
        say(f"could not start: {why}")
        say(f"  {note}")
        out(started=False, enabled=True, reason=why, session=session,
            claim_released=handed)
        board_post("note", f"spawn failed: {what} — {why}",
                   provenance(note), refs)
        return COULD_NOT_START

    say(f"started: {what} in {os.path.basename(repo_path)}")
    say(f"  session {session} — attach in tmux, or end it with `qb-end {session}`")
    out(started=True, enabled=True, policy=policy.where, command=command,
        number=number, session=session, repo_path=repo_path, pane=why,
        via=args.via,
        claim=None if kind is None else {"kind": kind, "value": number})
    return STARTED


def board_refs(repo_path: str, command: str, number: str | None) -> list[dict]:
    """What the board should render this post against. Best-effort throughout:
    a post with no refs is still a post."""
    refs: list[dict] = []
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
        from qbdata import repo_slug
        repo = repo_slug(repo_path)
    except Exception:                                             # noqa: BLE001
        repo = None
    if repo:
        refs.append({"kind": "repo", "value": repo})
    if number and re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+", str(number)) and command in SPAWNABLE:
        refs.append({"kind": SPAWNABLE[command].kind, "value": str(number),
                     **({"repo": repo} if repo else {})})
    return refs


def plan_has_free_item(repo_path: str) -> tuple[bool | None, str]:
    """Is there anything on this repo's plan to pick up? `(True|False|None, why)`.

    `None` is "could not tell" and is NOT a refusal — see the call site for why
    this one gate fails open. The board already computes the answer: `next` is the
    first item that is open, unclaimed, unblocked and not inside a plan somebody
    else is holding, so this is one read of a field that exists rather than a
    judgement made here.

    Scoped to the repo the spawn runs in, unlike `qbdata.fetch_plan`, which is
    deliberately fleet-wide because its callers are dashboards. The question here
    is about the checkout being spawned into, and a fleet-wide answer would say
    yes on the strength of an item in another repo.
    """
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
        from qbdata import board_client, repo_slug
        slug = repo_slug(repo_path)
        if not slug:
            return None, "this checkout has no repo slug"
        client, _cfg = board_client()
        # limit=1: the envelope carries `next` whatever the page holds, and the
        # items are not read here. Asking for the whole plan to look at one field
        # is a page nobody reads, on the hot path of every spawn.
        data = client.get("/plan", {"repo": slug, "limit": 1}) or {}
    except Exception as exc:                                      # noqa: BLE001
        return None, f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}"
    return bool(data.get("next")), ""


def agent_argv(command: str, number: str | None, policy: Policy,
               model: str | None, session: str) -> list[str]:
    """What the pane runs.

    The brief goes after `--`, the way `qb-seat` passes one and for its reason: it
    is read as a prompt whatever it starts with, and every brief here starts with
    a slash.

    `--session-id` is what makes the session endable before it has said anything.
    Without it the id is whatever the agent minted, known only once its
    SessionStart hook has told the board — a window in which the one thing nobody
    can do to a session started by a machine is stop it.

    Permissions are skipped by default and the argument is `qb-seat:650`'s,
    verbatim: a spawned window is a pane nobody is watching, so the first tool
    call wanting a permission the agent does not hold stops it dead in the one way
    this design cannot recover from — the pane looks busy, the board shows a live
    agent holding a claim, and nothing is moving. It is a real trade and it is
    made inside a file a human wrote on purpose; `"skip_permissions": false` in
    that file takes it back.
    """
    argv = [os.environ.get("QB_START_AGENT", "claude"), "--session-id", session]
    if policy.skip_permissions:
        argv.append("--dangerously-skip-permissions")
    if model:
        argv += ["--model", model]
    # The command alone when it takes no number (#541). Not `f"{command} "`
    # with a trailing space and not `{number or ""}`: the brief is read by a
    # slash-command parser, and "/get-involved " is a different string from
    # "/get-involved" to anything that matches on equality.
    brief = command if number is None else f"{command} {number}"
    return [*argv, "--", brief]


def spawn(command: str, number: str | None, policy: Policy, model: str | None,
          session: str, repo_path: str, via: str = DEFAULT_VIA) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """A detached tmux window, stamped so everything else can find it.

    Detached, and dropping to a shell afterwards, because `qb-dash-tui` already
    settled both: a window that stole the screen would interrupt whoever started
    it, and one that vanished on exit would take the only record of what happened
    with it.

    The pane writes `@qb_spawn_ended` on itself when the agent exits. That is the
    cap's honesty: the window deliberately outlives the agent, so the option is
    the only thing that can tell a session still running from its own transcript.
    """
    if not os.environ.get("TMUX"):
        return False, ("there is no tmux here — a spawn is a real pane somebody "
                       "can attach to, and this has nowhere to put one")
    shell = os.environ.get("SHELL", "/bin/bash")
    agent = " ".join(shlex.quote(a) for a in
                     agent_argv(command, number, policy, model, session))
    # `-t "$TMUX_PANE"`, and it is NOT belt and braces. `tmux set-option -p` with
    # no target does not mean "the pane I am running in": a command run from
    # inside a pane reaches the server as a client with no session of its own, so
    # the default target is the most recently used pane — somebody's seat, mid
    # turn, which would then be counted as a spawn that had ended. Measured while
    # writing this: the option landed on `%0` of another window every time.
    # `$TMUX_PANE` is exported into the pane and is the only handle it has on
    # itself.
    full = (f'{agent}; tmux set-option -p -t "$TMUX_PANE" {OPT_ENDED} 1 '
            f">/dev/null 2>&1; exec {shlex.quote(shell)} -i")
    name = f"{command.lstrip('/')}-{number}"
    made = tmux("new-window", "-d", "-P", "-F", "#{pane_id}",
                "-n", name, "-c", repo_path, full)
    if made.returncode != 0 or not made.stdout.strip():
        return False, f"tmux refused: {(made.stderr or '').strip()[:160] or 'no reason given'}"
    pane = made.stdout.strip()
    # Stamped after the fact, which leaves a sliver in which a concurrent
    # `qb-start` counts one spawn too few. Same non-atomicity `qb-admit`
    # documents about its own count-then-claim, and the same remedy is not
    # available: tmux has no way to create a window with options already on it.
    #
    # **AND THE WRITES ARE CHECKED**, which the codex review found they were not.
    # These three options are the whole of what makes a spawn findable: without
    # `@qb_session` the ✕ cannot end it and `qb-status` cannot see its pane, and
    # without `@qb_spawn` it is outside the cap for as long as it runs. A window
    # that got none of them is an agent nothing on the box can account for, so it
    # is closed rather than reported as started — the same judgement as refusing
    # a spawn whose claim could not be taken, one step later.
    for option, value in ((OPT_SPAWN, command), (OPT_SESSION, session),
                          (OPT_LABEL, name)):
        wrote = tmux("set-option", "-p", "-t", pane, option, value, timeout=5)
        if wrote.returncode != 0:
            why = (wrote.stderr or "").strip()[:120] or "no reason given"
            # And whether the close WORKED is reported. A `kill-pane` that failed
            # leaves an unstamped agent running against a claim about to be
            # handed back — the one state this whole file is arranged to prevent
            # — and saying "closed again" over it would hide precisely the thing
            # somebody has to go and do by hand.
            killed = tmux("kill-pane", "-t", pane, timeout=5)
            closed = ("closed again rather than left running where nothing could "
                      "count or end it" if killed.returncode == 0 else
                      f"AND COULD NOT BE CLOSED (kill-pane: "
                      f"{(killed.stderr or '').strip()[:80] or 'no reason given'}) "
                      f"— an unstamped agent is running in pane {pane} and only a "
                      f"human can stop it: `tmux kill-pane -t {pane}`")
            return False, (f"tmux made the window and would not stamp {option} on "
                           f"it ({why}) — {closed}")
    # And the provenance, AFTER the loop and unchecked, which is the whole of the
    # difference between the two: `tmux list-panes -F '#{@qb_spawn_via}'` answers
    # "what asked for this pane" for a window somebody found and does not
    # recognise, and it is worth having — but it is a label on a session, and the
    # board post above is the record that survives the pane being closed. See
    # OPT_VIA on why a spawn that got everything else is not thrown away for it.
    tmux("set-option", "-p", "-t", pane, OPT_VIA, via, timeout=5)
    return True, pane


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
